  geopolitics: "Day 267 of a closed Strait of Hormuz, and the weekend was the sharpest escalation in weeks. The UK Maritime Trade Operations agency reported two tankers struck separately by unknown projectiles on Saturday while exiting the strait, one hit in the stern and one in the engine room, with no casualties, and noted that one of them was running with its identification and navigation systems switched off in defiance of issued warnings and transit regulations. Iran's navy gave a different and unverifiable account, saying an oil tanker caught fire after striking a naval mine in a restricted area east of the strait. Iranian state television said Iran shot down a United States drone over Qeshm Island - the same island whose Islamic Revolutionary Guard Corps missile site American forces struck on Friday, which Iran said caused no serious damage. The United States has responded to Iran's shipping restrictions by blockading Iranian ports and deploying naval vessels to escort tankers through the waterway. And Qatar arrived in Tehran as mediator, after which Foreign Minister Abbas Araghchi said that discussing the Strait of Hormuz and its opening is pointless as long as Iran's demands are not met - which is the mediator being told the subject is closed. So the ledger now reads two commercial vessels hit in a single day, a direct military exchange between American and Iranian forces, a naval blockade, and diplomacy explicitly rejected. Set against that is a supply picture that has been winning for three weeks and did not change over the weekend. WTI settled at 83.41 dollars, up 1.18% on Friday but 14.14% lower on the month, and Brent at 92.57, up 0.64% and 14.93% lower, both on a third consecutive weekly decline. The International Energy Agency projects a global surplus near 2 million barrels a day this quarter and Goldman Sachs has warned of oversupply next year. The United States, China and Russia agreed at a trilateral meeting on Thursday to boost production and supply of refined petroleum products. The US Energy Secretary says flows through the strait have reached record levels as Middle Eastern supplies recover. And President Trump has said he is in no rush to decide on Iran despite reports of an increased likelihood of further strikes. The transmission into this board is therefore in three distinct places and it is worth keeping them separate. First, oil itself: the honest statement on Friday was that a market which would not bid a tanker fire had no premium left to unwind, so the asymmetry pointed up while the trend pointed down. The weekend doubled the input without changing the supply facts, which means Sunday's 23:00 futures open is the test - the gap risk is up, chasing it is the error, and USOIL stays a range to be traded on the retest rather than a long to be chased on the break. Second, the haven bid: this is the cleanest channel and it goes straight into the yen and the franc, both of which are already the two strongest currencies on the board for reasons of their own. The franc in particular has no policy story at all at a 0.00% rate with core inflation at 0.5%, so the Gulf is a large share of what it trades on, and its only cap is the Swiss National Bank's standing readiness to intervene. That is why the franc and yen crosses against the commodity bloc - AUD/CHF, AUD/JPY, NZD/CHF, CAD/JPY - are where the edge sits this week. Third, Europe's energy cost, which is a slower and more mechanical channel: EU natural gas at 88.45 euros rose 4.73% on Friday on the Troll field outage and lower Norwegian exports and is 192.90% higher than a year ago, with storage 69.8% full on November 19 against 81.2% a year earlier and colder Northwest European temperatures forecast. The Middle East war is directly implicated in that, with continued attacks on vessels near the strait threatening LNG shipments from the region. That gas price is the input behind a euro-area HICP of 4.0% and a near-certain December hike, and it is simultaneously a direct cost line for the DAX - which is why the euro's forward case and GER40's short case are the same fact pointing in two directions. The Canadian dollar is the fourth and most uncertain channel: a genuine oil repricing would repair the exact pass-through the October rate rise was aimed at and that WTI's 14.14% monthly fall has removed, which is why USD/CAD is sized down rather than pressed, and why Macklem on Tuesday at 19:25 matters more than its billing suggests.",
